The mechanisms are explicit, but observable impact requires deployed provider settings whose base URL contains `deepseek` and/or selected model identifiers containing `deepseek-r1`; no actual configuration values are supplied.

DeepSeek compatibility is broadened in two ways: OpenAI-provider configurations whose base URL mentions DeepSeek are directed to the standard OpenAI client before Azure detection, and both provider handlers now classify `deepseek-r1` model identifiers as DeepSeek reasoner models. The accompanying changeset describes this as an Azure DeepSeek fix.

Likely behavioral changes
  • When an OpenAI-provider base URL contains both `deepseek` and `azure.com`, client selection changes from `AzureOpenAI` to the standard `OpenAI` client because the DeepSeek condition now takes precedence over the Azure condition.
  • Both the dedicated DeepSeek handler and the generic OpenAI handler now classify model identifiers containing `deepseek-r1` as reasoner models, placing those models on the same existing reasoner-specific processing path as `deepseek-reasoner`.

